Recent advancements in natural language processing have made it possible for models to achieve high levels of performance in zero-shot settings using tailored prompts. However, creating effective prompts in a broad range of contexts remains a challenging task. To address this issue, our study proposes a semi-supervised approach which enables the transfer of prompt effectiveness to new scenarios through counterfactual reasoning. We developed and evaluated nine new selection tasks to test the reliability of existing high-performing prompts from eight different datasets, including six natural language inference datasets. Our analysis revealed that some prominent prompts are capable of achieving outstanding results in these new scenarios with minimal adaptation. Additionally, we found that prompt performance can be significantly enhanced by leveraging the data distribution information. The code and dataset have been made available for further study. 

